i='TCS-3857';_,c=card(i);refs=c['references'];refs[0]['locator']='Introduction, p. 27:4, the extracted question and its affirmative answer; Section 2, strict unit-sum utilities and Definitions 1–3; Section 3.2, Theorem 15 and the two examples on pp. 27:11–27:12. Published 5 December 2018.'
save(i,dict(title='Low-distortion cardinal versus ordinal mechanisms',formal='Historical question, answered affirmatively in the cited 2018 paper: can truthful cardinal voting mechanisms have distortion O(√(m log m)) while exhibiting behavior unavailable to truthful ordinal mechanisms? More precisely, there is a family of mechanisms μₙ,ₘ for all n≥1 voters and m≥3 alternatives, with a universal distortion bound C√(m log m), truthful in expectation, whose component for n=1 and m=3 violates both non-perversity and localization as defined below. These are the source’s concrete meanings of the extracted phrase “very different”. This is already a result of the source, not its neighboring open question about the optimal distortion.',definitions='Let A={1,…,m} and let Uₘ consist of vectors u∈[0,1]ᵐ with ∑ₐu(a)=1 and pairwise distinct coordinates. Reports and true utilities both range over Uₘ. A cardinal mechanism μ:Uₘⁿ→Δ(A) selects one alternative with its specified probabilities; there are no payments. Write μ(u)(a) for the probability of a. Truthfulness in expectation means that for every profile u, voter i and alternative report v∈Uₘ, ∑ₐuᵢ(a)μ(u)(a)≥∑ₐuᵢ(a)μ(u₋ᵢ,v)(a). Thus truth telling is a dominant strategy regardless of other reports. Social welfare is SWᵤ(a)=∑ᵢuᵢ(a). Distortion is the supremum over all u∈Uₘⁿ of maxₐSWᵤ(a)/∑ₐμ(u)(a)SWᵤ(a), with a zero denominator giving +∞. The constant C is independent of n,m and utilities; log may be taken as the natural logarithm. An ordinal mechanism depends only on the strict ranking induced by each utility vector. The source extends non-perversity to cardinal mechanisms by requiring that changing one report v to v′, with v′(a)>v(a) and the relative order of all alternatives other than a unchanged, never decreases the probability of a. Localization requires that whenever the same set B is the top k alternatives in v and v′ and ∑ₐ∈Bv(a)=∑ₐ∈Bv′(a), changing that one report preserves the total probability assigned to B. Both requirements quantify over all other voters’ fixed reports. A violation is a pair of legal reports and fixed other reports failing the corresponding inequality or equality. No running-time or finite-precision input condition is imposed by this structural existence question.',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='The affirmative result consists of a mechanism family with the uniform distortion bound and exact incentive guarantee, together with violations of both specified properties in its three-alternative, one-voter component. The cited Section 3.2 supplies this result. Improving the general optimal-distortion bounds is a separate question and is not needed to answer this historical card.',context_blocks=[dict(title='The question introduces a proved contribution',text='The saved sentence occurs immediately after a separate open distortion-gap question. Its next sentences announce the randomized hyperspherical mechanism and its affirmative answer to the structural comparison. Section 3.2 proves the welfare bound in Theorem 15 and exhibits failures of non-perversity and localization.',citation='primary'),dict(title='Information and incentives are different constraints',text='Cardinal reporting permits numerical utilities, but truthfulness still restricts which welfare guarantees a mechanism can achieve. The source proves a general Ω(√m) distortion lower bound and gives the O(√(m log m)) construction. Its structural result shows that this upper-bound scale does not force the two ordinal properties.',citation='primary'),dict(title='Normalization and randomization matter',text='The source uses strict, nonnegative, unit-sum utility vectors and dominant-strategy truthfulness in expectation. Unit-range utilities, approximate truthfulness and equilibrium welfare for manipulable mechanisms are different models, also discussed in the article.',citation='primary')],why='Clarifies whether good welfare under incentive constraints forces cardinal voting to inherit ordinal structure. The article already establishes the structural separation, so this record is historical rather than an active open benchmark problem.',references=refs,progress=[dict(date='2018-12-05',text='FSTTCS publication: Section 3.2 gives a truthful randomized cardinal mechanism with distortion O(√(m log m)); Theorem 15 and the following examples answer the extracted structural question.',citation='primary')],status='resolved',status_note='Read the complete primary article’s model, introductory question, Section 3.2 and Theorem 15, including both structural counterexamples. The source itself answers the extracted question. Searched for subsequent versions and work; no later result is required for this disposition. The neighboring optimal-distortion question is not substituted for this already answered one. No independent proof verification is claimed.',model_self_contained=True,requires_context=False,importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='The structural comparison isolates incentive constraints from the loss of numerical preference information, but the cited paper already answers it. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),review_note='Corrected an extraction false positive: the question introduces the paper’s affirmative contribution. Recovered strict unit-sum utilities, truthfulness in expectation and the structural properties; marked resolved.',working_summary=dict(sentences=['Cardinal voting accepts numerical utilities, whereas ordinal voting uses only rankings.','The card asks whether truthful cardinal mechanisms with good welfare can violate structural properties of ordinal mechanisms.','The original paper already answers yes with a randomized mechanism of distortion O(√(m log m)).','Its three-alternative examples violate both non-perversity and localization.','The adjacent question about the best possible distortion is separate; this extracted structural question is resolved.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary'])),notes=['Recognized the excerpt as an introduction to a proved result, not an open problem.','Recovered strict unit-sum utility domain, exact incentive constraints and O(sqrt(m log m)) distortion.','Matched Theorem15 and both structural examples; preserved the separate open distortion gap.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs]+['https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.FSTTCS.2018.27'])
p=DIRECTORY/'queue.json';q=json.loads(p.read_text());_,c=card(i)
